('128.5.b.2.B.xii',3,'The plural of luz is:','["luzes", "luces", "luzs", "lucez"]'::jsonb,1,'Change z to c before adding -es.','original'),
('128.5.b.2.B.xii',3,'The plural of lápiz is:','["lápizes", "lapices", "lápices", "lápizs"]'::jsonb,2,'Change z to c and add -es.','original'),
('128.5.b.2.C',2,'To alphabetize tiza, tigre and tina, which letter position decides the order?','["the word length", "the first letter", "the last letter", "the third letter"]'::jsonb,3,'All three share t and i, so compare the third letters.','original'),
('128.5.b.2.D',2,'Legible cursive means:','["others can read your letters", "letters are as tiny as possible", "letters slant in every direction", "words touch each other"]'::jsonb,0,'Legibility is the goal of handwriting.','original'),
('128.5.b.2.D',2,'In cursive writing, the space between words should be:','["none at all", "about the width of one letter", "as wide as a line", "different every time"]'::jsonb,1,'Appropriate spacing keeps words separate.','original'),
('128.5.b.3.A',2,'Which dictionary feature helps you say a word aloud?','["the page number", "the word count", "the pronunciation guide", "the cover title"]'::jsonb,2,'Entries show pronunciation and syllable breaks.','original'),
('128.5.b.3.A',2,'A dictionary shows syllabication so you can:','["shorten the word", "find the author", "change the meaning", "divide the word into syllables"]'::jsonb,3,'Syllable breaks help with decoding and spelling.','original'),
('128.5.b.3.B',3,'Reading the sentences before and after a hard word gives you:','["clues to its meaning", "the author''s age", "its spelling only", "the page number"]'::jsonb,0,'Context beyond the sentence can define a word.','original'),
('128.5.b.3.B',3,'The cat is ágil. It jumps over fences quickly. What does ágil most likely mean?','["very hungry", "quick and graceful", "slow and sleepy", "very old"]'::jsonb,1,'Context shows the cat moves quickly.','original'),
('128.5.b.3.C',3,'The prefix in- in the word incapaz means:','["again", "above", "not", "before"]'::jsonb,2,'In- can mean not.','original'),
('128.5.b.3.C',3,'The suffix -dad in bondad forms a noun meaning:','["a person who is good", "a place for good things", "very good", "the quality of being good"]'::jsonb,3,'-dad makes nouns for qualities.','original'),
('128.5.b.3.D',3,'Which pair of words are antonyms?','["alto and bajo", "rápido and veloz", "bonito and lindo", "grande and enorme"]'::jsonb,0,'Antonyms have opposite meanings.','original'),
('128.5.b.3.D',4,'The idiom ''tomar el pelo'' means:','["to take hair", "to joke with or tease someone", "to cut hair", "to wash hair"]'::jsonb,1,'An idiom''s meaning is not literal.','original'),
('128.5.b.3.E',3,'Which pair sounds the same but has different spellings?','["casa and cosa", "mesa and masa", "tubo and tuvo", "pato and pata"]'::jsonb,2,'Words that sound alike with different spellings are homophones.','original'),
('128.5.b.3.E',3,'A homograph is a word that:','["rhymes with other words", "sounds the same and means the same", "has an opposite meaning", "is spelled the same but has different meanings"]'::jsonb,3,'Homographs share spelling but not meaning.','original'),
('128.5.b.4',2,'Practicing listening and speaking helps reading and writing because:','["the language skills support each other", "only speaking counts", "they are unrelated", "only reading matters"]'::jsonb,0,'Foundational language skills develop together.','original'),
('128.5.b.4',2,'A student listens to a story and then writes a summary. Which two skills worked together?','["speaking and drawing", "listening and writing", "reading and tracing", "singing and counting"]'::jsonb,1,'Skills are combined in real tasks.','original'),
('128.5.b.5',2,'Which activity builds both reading and speaking?','["sorting buttons", "silent coloring", "reading a story aloud with a partner and discussing it", "copying a menu"]'::jsonb,2,'Reading aloud and discussion combine skills.','original'),
('128.5.b.5',2,'A student can strengthen second-language skills by:','["only listening for years", "only memorizing lists", "avoiding practice", "using the language in all four skill areas daily"]'::jsonb,3,'Regular use of listening, speaking, reading and writing builds fluency.','original'),
('128.5.b.6.A',2,'A purpose for reading a science article could be:','["to learn facts about a topic", "to avoid the title", "to count the commas", "to find the longest word"]'::jsonb,0,'Readers set a purpose before reading.','original'),
('128.5.b.6.A',2,'Choosing a book on your own to read for fun makes it a:','["assigned text", "self-selected text", "backward text", "restricted text"]'::jsonb,1,'Self-selected texts are chosen by the reader.','original'),
('128.5.b.6.B',3,'Which question deepens understanding before reading?','["How many pages are left?", "Who can read faster?", "What do I already know about this topic?", "Where is my backpack?"]'::jsonb,2,'Pre-reading questions activate prior knowledge.','original'),
('128.5.b.6.B',3,'After reading, a good question to ask is:','["Who is sitting next to me?", "Where is the dictionary?", "What time is it?", "How did the main character change?"]'::jsonb,3,'Questions after reading deepen comprehension.','original'),
('128.5.b.6.C',3,'Using the title and pictures to guess what a text is about is:','["making a prediction", "alphabetizing", "editing", "summarizing the end"]'::jsonb,0,'Predictions use text features.','original'),
('128.5.b.6.C',3,'You predicted a happy ending but the story ended sadly. The best move is to:','["blame the author", "correct your prediction using the text", "reread nothing", "ignore the ending"]'::jsonb,1,'Readers confirm or correct predictions with evidence.','original'),
('128.5.b.6.D',2,'Picturing a story scene in your mind helps you to:','["read without thinking", "skip words", "understand details better", "ignore the plot"]'::jsonb,2,'Mental images deepen understanding.','original'),
('128.5.b.6.D',2,'Which words help you create a mental image?','["the of it", "it was there", "and then", "sparkling blue waves crashing on the shore"]'::jsonb,3,'Descriptive details support imagery.','original'),
('128.5.b.6.E',3,'When a story reminds you of another book, you are making a:','["text-to-text connection", "text-to-self connection", "caption", "spelling rule"]'::jsonb,0,'Connections can link texts to other texts.','original'),
('128.5.b.6.E',3,'A story about recycling reminds you of a news report about trash. That is a:','["glossary entry", "text-to-world connection", "text-to-self connection", "rhyming pattern"]'::jsonb,1,'Connections can link texts to society.','original'),
('128.5.b.6.F',3,'The text says Mia slammed the door and stomped upstairs. You can infer she is:','["sleepy", "thrilled", "angry", "lost"]'::jsonb,2,'Actions give clues about feelings.','original'),
('128.5.b.6.F',3,'An inference combines text evidence with:','["the book''s weight", "the page number", "a random guess", "what you already know"]'::jsonb,3,'Inferences use clues plus background knowledge.','original'),
('128.5.b.6.G',3,'Which detail is most important in a paragraph about how bees make honey?','["Bees collect nectar and turn it into honey.", "Some bees are yellow.", "The paragraph has five sentences.", "The page is near the back."]'::jsonb,0,'Key ideas are the details that carry the main point.','original'),
('128.5.b.6.G',3,'Evaluating details helps you find the:','["page count", "key idea", "publisher", "font size"]'::jsonb,1,'Weighing details reveals what matters most.','original'),
('128.5.b.6.H',3,'Combining facts from two articles to build a new idea is:','["skimming", "decoding", "synthesizing", "scanning"]'::jsonb,2,'Synthesis creates new understanding.','original'),
('128.5.b.6.H',3,'After reading about frogs in a book and in a video, you write one new conclusion. You are:','["spelling words", "skipping details", "copying a page", "synthesizing information"]'::jsonb,3,'Merging sources into a new conclusion is synthesis.','original'),
('128.5.b.6.I',3,'Your comprehension drops in the middle of a chapter. A fix-up strategy is to:','["re-read the confusing part", "close the book for good", "skip to the last page", "read faster"]'::jsonb,0,'Monitoring means adjusting when you do not understand.','original'),
('128.5.b.6.I',3,'Using what you already know about a topic to understand a hard paragraph is:','["skipping the paragraph", "using background knowledge", "memorizing letters", "guessing blindly"]'::jsonb,1,'Background knowledge is a comprehension strategy.','original'),
('128.5.b.7.A',2,'Telling how a character''s move reminds you of your own is:','["a rhyme scheme", "a table of contents", "a personal connection", "a glossary term"]'::jsonb,2,'Personal connections link texts to your life.','original'),
('128.5.b.7.A',2,'Which sentence describes a personal connection?','["The author lives in Texas.", "The book has ten chapters.", "The cover is blue.", "This reminds me of when I moved to a new school."]'::jsonb,3,'A personal connection links the text to your experience.','original'),
('128.5.b.7.B',3,'A strong written response to a text:','["shows you understood the main points", "has no details", "lists unrelated facts", "repeats the title only"]'::jsonb,0,'Responses demonstrate understanding.','original'),
('128.5.b.7.B',3,'Which opening for a response shows understanding?','["I did not read it.", "The story is mostly about a girl who learns to be brave.", "The story is long.", "It has pictures."]'::jsonb,1,'A response names the main idea.','original'),
('128.5.b.7.C',3,'Which detail is text evidence that Ana is generous?','["Ana is in third grade.", "Ana likes the color green.", "Ana gave half her lunch to a classmate.", "Ana has a brother."]'::jsonb,2,'Evidence is a detail from the text that supports a claim.','original'),
('128.5.b.7.C',3,'Strong text evidence is:','["a feeling alone", "a guess", "an unrelated fact", "a detail taken from the text"]'::jsonb,3,'Evidence comes from the text itself.','original'),
('128.5.b.7.D',3,'Which sentence paraphrases ''The tiny pup trembled in the cold''?','["The small puppy shook because it was chilly.", "The pup trembled in the cold.", "The huge dog jumped in the sun.", "The cat slept."]'::jsonb,0,'A paraphrase keeps the meaning in new words.','original'),
('128.5.b.7.D',3,'A retelling that keeps logical order tells the events in:','["only the last page", "the beginning, middle and end", "the title and nothing else", "middle first, then beginning"]'::jsonb,1,'Retelling maintains order.','original'),
('128.5.b.7.E',2,'Annotating a text means:','["tearing the pages", "covering it", "marking it and writing notes on it", "erasing it"]'::jsonb,2,'Annotation is interaction with a source.','original'),
('128.5.b.7.E',2,'Which is a way to interact with a source?','["closing it unread", "hiding it", "turning it upside down", "taking notes while reading"]'::jsonb,3,'Notetaking is meaningful interaction.','original'),
('128.5.b.7.F',3,'The class learned the word ''curious''. Which sentence uses it?','["The curious puppy sniffed every corner.", "A curious of four.", "Curious curious curious.", "The curious is a table."]'::jsonb,0,'Use new vocabulary correctly in context.','original'),
('128.5.b.7.F',3,'Using newly learned words in your response shows that you:','["forgot them", "understand and can apply them", "skipped them", "dislike reading"]'::jsonb,1,'Application shows learning.','original'),
('128.5.b.7.G',3,'Which discussion comment points to an important idea?','["The bell rings soon.", "I''m hungry.", "The author says teamwork helped them win.", "My shoes are new."]'::jsonb,2,'Important ideas relate to the meaning of the text.','original'),
('128.5.b.7.G',3,'When discussing ideas important to the meaning, you should:','["talk about unrelated things", "stay silent", "only repeat the title", "point to specific parts of the text"]'::jsonb,3,'Discussion is supported by the text.','original'),
('128.5.b.8.A',3,'A story about a lost dog teaches that honesty matters. ''Honesty matters'' is the:','["theme", "topic", "title", "setting"]'::jsonb,0,'A theme is a message, not just a subject.','original')
) as v(code, diff, prompt, choices, ans, expl, src)
